# IAM Identity Policies

```text
Identity policies are attached to AWS identities and either
ALLOW or DENY access to AWS resources.

It is a set of security statements for AWS. It grants access or denies access to
AWS products to any identity which uses that policy.

IAM Policy Documents are made using JSON.
```

> **At A high level a IAM Policy Document is just one or more statements**

---

## So what makes up a statement?

### 1. Statement ID or SID

```text
- Optional, lets you identify a statement and what it does.
- Important to always use this regardless of how big or small a policy document is.
```

### Action & Resource

```text
Every Interaction you have with AWS is a combination of two main things,
    1. Resource you're interacting with
    2. The Actions you're attempting to perform on that resource.

A Statement only applies if the interaction that you're having with AWS match
the action and the resource.

The actual action part of a statement matches one or more actions.

One Action could map to multiple Actions.

It CAN be very specific and list a specific individual action.

The format is :
    Service: Operation

Wildcards can be used and they can be used to match any, inside a
service by doing something like...
    "Service:*"

Alternatively actions can be a list of individual actions.

So you have effectively 3 options:
    1. Specific Action
    2. Wild Card
    3. A list of multiple independent actions

Resources is the same only it matches AWS resources.
If you plan on using the List format or wildcard, Use the ARN format(Amazon resource )
```

### Effect

```text
Effect is either ALLOW/DENY Only.
It controls what AWS does if the action and the resource parts of the statement
match the operation that you're attempting to do with AWS.

Eg: If you're trying to access a S3 Bucket and the action and resource part of the
statement match and the EFFECT is ALLOWED then you'll be allowed to do it.

If you're on DENY then it will PREVENT the action from going through.
```

---

## There will be cases when you will have broad allows/denies

```text
Basically, You could have a broad allow but have specific actions or resources be Denied.

It's kinda up to you to figure that out.

Eg: Resource *, Action S3:* , Effect: Allow

This basically allows you do anything remove,build,alter, S3 buckets.
(S3 Administrator basically)

Continuing this..

    There could be a second statement.

    resource:arn:aws:s3:::catgifs, Action:s3:*, Effect: Deny

    This would deny catgifs bucket and any objects in that bucket.

    Both have their own UID.
```

---

## Simple Rules to not get confused

```text
1. Explicit Denies
    If you have a statement that EXPLICITLY DENIES something then
    it wins over EVERYTHING.

2. Explicit Allows
    If you have a statement that EXPLICITLY ALLOWS access to a resource then it
    usually goes through unless you have an explicit deny.

    If you are explicitly allowed and denied a specific resource then DENY will ALWAYS win.

3. Implicit Deny
    If there is no deny/allow, then the default goes through,
    It just by default denies you from everything.

If they have no explicit access, they'll have no access.
```

> Using a managed policy would make things easier so you can just add people to that one instead of dealing with multiple policies at once.

