## EXAMPLE

```text
Sally - Developer
Miles - Accounts
Mike  - Production
```

### Option 1 : Inline Policy

```text
You could design a policy that grants access to the resources needed for that project.
Then, assign the JSON for each account.

This was called Inline Policy.
    You are applying the policy to each user individually.

    This would work in this case, but it's not best case due it being difficult to maintain.

    Changing the access rights means changing it on all 3 inline policies.

    Meaning it doesn't scale properly.
```

### Option 2 : Managed Policy

```text
You create an object policy. You then give it to any users you want it to apply to.

This is called a Managed Policy

    This is good because it's reusable, and Low Management Overhead.
    (JSON is easy to change because one change affects all users).

    Scalable.

    This should be the default operational rights.
```

---

## When would you use an inline policy then?

```text
This is usually for special or exceptions. You can tune it to block certain actions
for a certain user or allow a user to something.
```
